<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  Fast DDS transport profile for appIVI: UDPv4 only, no shared memory.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  ===============
  appIVI runs as the `weston` user. Every ROS 2 publisher it subscribes to
  (cuda_pointpillars_node and the rest of the perception pipeline) runs as
  `root`. Fast DDS creates its shared-memory segments and port ring buffers
  under /dev/shm using the creating process's umask, which yields mode 0644:

      -rw-r--r-- root   root   fastrtps_port7421          <- root publisher
      -rw-r--r-- weston weston fastrtps_port7417          <- appIVI
      -rw-r--r-- root   root   sem.fastrtps_port7421_mutex

  Whichever user creates a port owns it, and the other user then holds only
  read access. A RELIABLE reader has to write ACKNACKs back into the writer's
  port, so `weston` cannot complete the SHM channel to a root-owned publisher.

  The failure is silent and looks like anything but a transport problem:
  discovery runs over UDP multicast and succeeds, so `ros2 topic info -v`
  reports the subscription MATCHED, with a stable GID and fully compatible QoS
  on both ends -- while not a single sample is ever delivered and the app's ROS
  spin thread sits at zero CPU.

  MEASURED
  ========
  Identical `ros2 topic hz /object_detections_3d`, same publisher, same instant:

      as root    -> 9.6 Hz
      as weston  -> nothing at all
      as weston, with this profile -> 9.6 Hz

  It is intermittent across boots only because it depends on which user's
  participant created the shared port first, which is why restarting the IVI
  sometimes appeared to fix it and sometimes did not.

  COST
  ====
  Shared memory is genuinely faster than loopback UDP, but not measurably so at
  this traffic. Measured on /object_detections_3d: mean message 2.5-2.8 KB (max
  3.98 KB), ~20 KB/s. One UDP datagram per message, no fragmentation. The SHM
  advantage on a 2.5 KB payload is on the order of tens of microseconds, against
  a 104 ms message period at 9.6 Hz -- roughly 0.05% of it, while the detector
  itself spends 31 ms/frame and the IVI's DetectionSmoother interpolates at
  60 Hz regardless. Zero-copy pays off on megabyte payloads; this is not one.

  Nothing heavy is affected, because this profile is scoped to appIVI alone via
  FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE in ivi-app.service. The bulk path --
  rosbag2_player / the lidar driver feeding ~1.9 MB point clouds at ~19 MB/s
  into cuda_pointpillars_node -- is root-to-root and keeps shared memory
  untouched, as does the camera/YOLO pipeline. Publishers keep their default
  transports; a participant that advertises only UDP locators is simply reached
  over UDP, so nothing else on the board has to change.

  REVISIT IF: appIVI ever subscribes to a point cloud or camera stream again
  (the cloud subscription was deliberately removed -- see the long comment in
  DriveView/src/ros_node.cpp). At megabyte payloads this tradeoff flips, and the
  right answer becomes fixing the SHM permissions instead: give every publisher
  AND this app UMask=0000 so the /dev/shm files land 0666. That was rejected
  here because it is fragile -- the perception pipeline is often launched by
  hand from a root shell, whose default umask 022 silently recreates root-owned
  0644 ports and breaks the channel again.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>udp_only_tp</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="udp_only_participant" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>udp_only_tp</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <!-- Without this, the builtin SHM+UDP pair is added back alongside
             userTransports and the SHM locators reappear. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
  </profiles>
</dds>
